From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 00:39:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 013/159] [ae-patch] Fix Run on chat code blocks: paste timing,
 shell kind, transcripts, streaming, focus

Six bugs in the Run button (patch 0011), found in review after it merged:

- A Run pasted as soon as the terminal's WebSocket opened, before the shell's
  first output was parsed, so xterm did not yet know the shell wanted
  bracketed paste. Every newline went as Enter: the lines of a block held back
  for an open heredoc ran anyway, and a password prompt could read the next
  line. A sink is now ready only once the shell has drawn and gone quiet, and
  a multi-line block is never pasted unbracketed (nothing is sent; a toast
  says why).
- A powershell block was pasted into bash on a Linux host. Blocks now run only
  in a shell of their kind (read from the terminal's spec name); a powershell
  block shows Run only when the session declares a PowerShell shell.
- A `$ ` line anywhere in a bash block (say inside a heredoc body) turned the
  block into a transcript and only that line was sent; a prompted heredoc lost
  its body; a console block with no `$ ` prompt sent its output lines as
  commands. Only a console fence or a prompted first line makes a transcript,
  the lines a prompted command leaves open are kept (their `> ` prompt
  stripped), and a console block without a prompt gets no Run.
- Run showed on a block still streaming, so a partial command could be
  confirmed. It now waits for the message to finish (Streamdown's own context
  carries the mode).
- After a Run the focus did not reach the terminal, so a sudo password typed
  straight away went nowhere. The terminal is focused once the sheet closes.
- On a phone the confirm sheet could grow past the screen with the Run button
  out of reach. It now scrolls inside 85vh, like upstream's other dialogs.

TerminalSession's Run hooks change shape (aeOnOutput, aeBracketedPaste,
aeWrite replace aePaste); the logic stays in the AE files.

